---
import PageLayout from '../../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../../../components/PageHero.astro';
import SplitSection from '../../../components/SplitSection.astro';
import LinkCards from '../../../components/LinkCards.astro';
import CTABand from '../../../components/CTABand.astro';
import { autresMarches } from '../../../data/marches';
import humaineImg from '../../../assets/img/app-alimentation-humaine.webp';

const autres = autresMarches('human-food', 'en').map((m) => ({
  href: `/en/applications/${m.slug}`,
  titre: m.titre,
  texte: m.texte,
  image: m.image,
  alt: m.alt,
}));
---

<PageLayout
  title="Human food — TVP, infant flours, snacks, breakfast cereals | SETREM"
  description="Four families of highly nutritious foods produced by extrusion: textured vegetable proteins, infant flours, snacks, breakfast cereals."
  path="/en/applications/human-food"
>
  <PageHero
    kicker="Applications"
    titre="Human food"
    lede="Four families of products, a single cooking process: textured vegetable proteins, infant food, snacks and breakfast cereals."
  />

  <section class="band sheet" aria-label="The product families">
    <div class="container">
      {/* Le chapô de la page énumère déjà les quatre familles : ce bloc
          pose l'enjeu et n'y revient pas. */}
      <SplitSection
        kicker="What is at stake"
        titre="Feeding a growing population."
        image={humaineImg}
        alt="Extruded human food products"
      >
        <p>
          With the rapid growth of the world population, food is a major
          challenge: solutions must be found to meet everyone's nutritional
          needs. Extrusion cooking is one of those levers — it turns simple
          raw materials into highly nutritious foods, stable and ready to use.
        </p>
      </SplitSection>

      <SplitSection
        kicker="Family 01"
        titre="Textured vegetable proteins (TVP)."
      >
        <p>
          On average, 4.9&nbsp;kg of vegetable protein is needed to produce
          1&nbsp;kg of animal protein. It is therefore necessary to produce
          vegetable proteins that can replace animal proteins, to ensure
          sustainable global food security. Extrusion cooking makes it
          possible to turn vegetable proteins into a meat analogue known as
          textured vegetable protein (TVP).
        </p>
        <p>
          They were created to have the same sensory characteristics as animal
          proteins: appearance, texture, mouthfeel. They allow all or part of
          the meat in a dish to be replaced — bolognese sauce, nuggets,
          burgers, sausages — but are also eaten as a dish in their own right
          in many countries such as China, Taiwan, Japan and South Korea.
        </p>
        <h3>The advantages of TVP</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            they suit the dietary restrictions of various religions, cultures
            and forms of vegetarianism,
          </li>
          <li>lower cost than animal proteins,</li>
          <li>long shelf life,</li>
          <li>easy to use.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          All vegetable proteins can be textured, but soybeans are by far the
          most widely used in the manufacture of TVP: supplies are plentiful
          and the price is low.
        </p>
      </SplitSection>

      <SplitSection kicker="Family 02" titre="Infant food.">
        <p>
          Extrusion cooking makes it possible to produce ready-to-use powders
          to which water alone need be added to obtain instantly rehydrated
          food, fully gelatinised and cooked. The extruded blend is sterile:
          it is wholesome and safe for children. Care must nevertheless be
          taken over the quality of the water or milk added, as well as over
          the storage conditions of the product once rehydrated.
        </p>
        <p>
          These porridges play an important role in the food independence of
          developing countries: highly energetic and low in cost, they are a
          means of fighting child malnutrition. Extrusion makes it possible to
          use any raw material — each country can therefore adapt its recipes
          to locally available resources. SETREM also adapts its machines to
          the energy availability and environmental constraints of the
          country.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/en/expert-insights/infant-flour-production-by-extrusion-cooking">
            Read the article: producing infant flours by extrusion cooking →
          </a>
        </p>
      </SplitSection>

      <SplitSection
        kicker="Families 03 & 04"
        titre="Snacks and breakfast cereals."
      >
        {/* « Les formes extrudées sont très variées afin de répondre à
            toutes les sollicitations du marché » ouvrait aussi le bloc
            petfood, mot pour mot : gardé là-bas, reformulé ici. */}
        <p>
          A snack can take almost any shape: potato- or maize-based doughs
          formed to shape, directly expanded extrudates, co-extruded
          products. Extrusion makes it possible to give snacks different
          properties depending on the audience:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>low in calories, containing resistant starch,</li>
          <li>refined in taste for adults,</li>
          <li>attractive in shape and colour for children.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Widespread in North America and Central Europe, breakfast cereals
          represent a fast-growing market: 10 to 20% in Italy, 20 to
          25% in Eastern Europe, 15 to 20% in Latin America.
          Manufacturers mainly target three audiences: children, consumers
          concerned with nutrition and health, and consumers looking for
          products high in energy. As with snacks, extrusion makes it possible
          to produce a whole range of products suited to the expectations of
          these different audiences.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/en/solutions/equipment-capacity-chart">
            See the capacity chart →
          </a>
        </p>
      </SplitSection>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--raised band--tight" aria-labelledby="autres-titre">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="band__head">
        <h2 class="band__title reveal" id="autres-titre">The other markets</h2>
      </div>
      <LinkCards items={autres} cols={3} cta="View the market" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand />
</PageLayout>
